 "title": "Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction (2nd edition)",
 "byline": "Richard S. Sutton and Andrew G. Barto \u00b7 MIT Press, 2018 (authors\u2019 free PDF, 2020 printing)",
 "note": "PDF page = printed page + 22. Chapter summaries and bibliographical remarks are not broken out; the references and index are not broken down. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "The standard introduction to reinforcement learning: an agent learning by trial and error to maximize reward. Part I treats the tabular case exactly: bandits and exploration, finite MDPs and Bellman equations, dynamic programming, Monte Carlo methods, TD learning (Sarsa, Q-learning, Expected Sarsa, double learning), n-step bootstrapping and planning with learned models (Dyna, prioritized sweeping, rollouts, MCTS). Part II brings in function approximation: on-policy prediction and control (including neural networks, LSTD and average reward), the difficulties of off-policy learning and the deadly triad, eligibility traces with true online TD(\u03bb), and policy-gradient and actor\u2013critic methods. Part III connects RL to animal learning psychology and to dopamine and neuroscience, presents case studies from TD-Gammon to AlphaGo, and closes with open frontiers."
}